#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498612} :activity/id #uuid "cfe100cb-7a41-57b9-9543-1db28653961c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34800481-715e-5257-9d4c-7d037cac6fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}y+yz+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497915} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf158fb-4d25-5864-9cb6-eb9d3f393756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498152} {:answer/id #uuid "cd789499-1240-527d-ba30-e55b7342f064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+yz+xz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498261} {:answer/id #uuid "cdace420-7534-506a-9578-b4e2449a1989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}z+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498262} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ae4394-1680-5b98-a6cf-4a436850cd18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498152} :answer-field/id #uuid "2575d212-1190-514e-a919-fd516555543d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "556e4e71-69c2-58ca-8978-843d74c13525" :question/math-academy-id "q-132102" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=4xy\\,i+(2{x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 2yz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & ={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f7f665e-6a64-51bb-995b-e3a8d6ce7d2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05edf187-268d-5875-9d08-b64af6b5dd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (1,3)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497810} {:answer/id #uuid "14d70569-8f49-5404-9062-9c804d3bef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497847} {:answer/id #uuid "67b7ea21-30b5-51b0-bc0d-9e0e21660aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (1,3)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498048} {:answer/id #uuid "861ce0bd-d66a-5cf3-bfa2-91bac6c00c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498109} {:answer/id #uuid "cb519f8f-9af4-58e7-bde3-840dbf421c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498109} :answer-field/id #uuid "2add9653-97c2-5314-8eb6-5cd64b3d830e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c17f34a-606d-54c2-a49c-4cce7dcaf8e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80720" :question/problem "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ {2}^{xy},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<3,\\,0<y<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(-1,3)\\times (0,2)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =((-1,1)\\cup (1,3))\\times (0,2) \\\\  & =((-1,1)\\times (0,2))\\cup ((1,3)\\times (0,2)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(-1,1)\\times (0,2)}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\text{ }+{∬}_{(1,3)\\times (0,2)}{2}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "10c837f4-3881-5f94-911e-e3ff56ee65c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5011f4d0-0c40-5764-9ee2-dff49de39782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437261} {:answer/id #uuid "639601fb-cc44-52a3-bcb1-718c744fd6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437262} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb75712-99aa-5d77-a9d1-0d236df66a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437264} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} {:answer/id #uuid "f132c264-24c8-5558-941c-11fddb4def73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c152aa95-4d94-5d9c-b5bb-30ac91e12518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1c164545-5826-5a3e-806d-b0dc3b18d6d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b25027-8221-5098-9167-254d753c3213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497909} {:answer/id #uuid "4f11998a-ad24-5209-8a9f-c63c403858e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497989} {:answer/id #uuid "5bda7aff-8f4a-54a5-aebf-3a9be4dd94f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498017} {:answer/id #uuid "9a175c4f-3333-5a30-a0ee-942d881a95f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498148} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8dc81a-a234-558b-87aa-921ac69d0c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498017} :answer-field/id #uuid "594aa2c5-6ab3-59e0-94ef-12b55bd75a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "023c52b0-f119-5895-8d3f-31a9468a20b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130768" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=6.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e6ea2d9-94f3-582a-af54-8a84e901dd4b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e0441d-7176-5665-92dd-c26fbcb98df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} {:answer/id #uuid "09d94921-07ab-5e03-a01d-a08146f1581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288110} {:answer/id #uuid "6fad7b2e-173a-553d-a0c3-c112c2827a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288807} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fc6f2a-51d8-5a81-9055-de9f6ce7af3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289461} {:answer/id #uuid "efe4bf53-696a-5f8e-b619-8486266ab583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4b747c-3e97-5b8d-86b0-f905f174bf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21fdd56a-1bd5-5736-b8c0-5f4753ddc47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-225166" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(1-u-v),\\,y=\\frac{1-v}{1+u}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,⇒\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/ff/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b56955ce-7d3d-51f9-aa6c-775bc47e70ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6641cc50-250b-56ad-b14a-c1124ec8c662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245216} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd41642-b280-5a7c-b432-e2ff0d315b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245311} {:answer/id #uuid "a10427df-c462-55bc-928a-4193f572fa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245747} {:answer/id #uuid "c751a88a-3aa0-5648-a5b6-305258fb0889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246079} {:answer/id #uuid "dbad1c26-6ccc-544d-abe3-93664ba57768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245747} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec8a63e-d3c0-5582-b9c7-8a8870cbc79b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "438d91ee-b10a-5c27-8e0b-9fc621fd3a05" :question/math-academy-id "q-112208" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+3\\cdot {b}_{2}+6\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "fb66811b-e2d4-56e3-a240-443861ad0b1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498622}}] :activity/title "Quiz 15" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497794}]}
